  m_text: Enterococcus faecium (VRE) marches as a tight chain of tough little cells
    that survive almost anywhere. It lives quietly in the gut but turns dangerous
    during long hospital stays, striking catheters and the bloodstream. Its armor
    was built to defeat vancomycin, the weapon once trusted against every gram-positive
    foe. Only a newer class of antibiotic can crack its defenses.
